IBM ACE 12 offers two powerful tools for managing time-sensitive tasks in your message flows: Timeout Control and Timeout Notification nodes. These dynamic duo work together to orchestrate events at specific times or regular intervals, ensuring your flows run smoothly and efficiently.

Part 1
Timeout Notification
The attentive listener, this node receives the timeout requests from its paired controllers, processes them, and forwards the information. You choose what gets sent on — the entire message or just specific parts. Think of it as the announcer, broadcasting the scheduled events to the rest of your flow, triggering subsequent actions.


It needs a unique identifier (any unique string) to run.

In operation mode:

Automatic-if you need to use timeout node as a standalone node.

Controlled-Want to use timeout notification with timeout control node.

Time interval (sec)-after how much interval of time i want my message flow to be triggered. e.g. after every 10 seconds, my flow will be triggered.


Note : You first add a breakpoint, launch debugger, then deploy it.


If you hit terminate, it’ll take 10 seconds (Time interval) to trigger again automatically.


There is no message since we’re just triggering.

Automatically, Timeout node sends information to LocalEnvironment.

Also, if we want to set LocalEnvironment, we set some information in Output LocalEnvironment for immediate next node.

Let’s implement a simple requirement. We need to connect to a database. Client want to change Timeout interval as per different environments. You can’t deploy different versions of code in different environment. Example, the timeout interval was 20 and he/she wants us to change to 50 without making changes to our flow.

The solution: Create a configurable service. A configurable service allows us to make some changes at runtime (we can change some property without re-deploying my app or making changes to our flow).

Open console to create configurable service.


aggregationnode…integration node name
-c…your configurable service type (Timer)
-o…you set it as default or enter unique identifier name i.e. TN_01
-n…parameter you want to change (timeoutIntervalSeconds)
-v…the value (50)
